I’ll inspect the measurement metadata, pattern, and the provided PXRD workflow/tooling first. Then I’ll identify candidate phases, build and refine a GSAS‑II model, and validate that every submitted artifact is internally consistent.

The sample is a Cu Kα scan of an 800 °C WO₃ + Mn₃O₄ reaction, so plausible phases include Mn tungstates, residual oxides, and off-stoichiometric Mn–W–O intermediates. I’m now separating peak families and searching the frozen reference library by structure/composition, not assuming the nominal 1:1 product is the only phase.

The dominant peak set matches monoclinic wolframite MnWO₄ very closely, including the diagnostic 29.98/30.42° doublet and its full high-angle sequence. Several strong unmatched lines—especially near 20.39, 23.10, 26.56, 28.60, 33–35°—indicate at least one secondary phase; I’m testing WO₃ polymorphs and residual Mn oxide jointly so overlapping peaks do not bias the fractions.

The three-phase trial confirms MnWO₄ and monoclinic WO₃, but it also exposes a separate, substantial peak family that ordinary tetragonal Mn₃O₄ cannot explain (notably 19.86/20.39, 28.60, 34.12/35.09, 46–47°). I’m treating this as a likely off-stoichiometric Mn–W–O phase and screening structural analogues before assigning it; forcing Mn₃O₄ into those peaks makes its cell and broadening diverge unphysically.

The unmatched phase is chemically constrained as well: an Mn-rich tungstate paired with WO₃ is the natural way to preserve the stated 1:1 Mn:W feed. I’m indexing that residual family and checking Mn₂WO₅/Mn₃WO₆-type models; this is more consistent with the data than assigning the residual to a manganese-only oxide.

A chemically credible analogue has emerged: monoclinic Co₃TeO₆ maps directly to Mn₃WO₆ by the two isovalent substitutions Co²⁺→Mn²⁺ and Te⁶⁺→W⁶⁺, preserves sensible metal–oxygen distances, and matches all nine diagnostic residual lines after only ~1% cell scaling. I’m now testing that atomic model in Rietveld refinement against the alternatives, rather than accepting peak-position coincidence alone.

The Co₃TeO₆-derived Mn₃WO₆ model is physically sound but only improves the preliminary Rwp from about 20.5% to 19.4%, so it is not yet a convincing assignment. I’m comparing it against Mn₂WO₅ and MnW₂O₇ analogue models under the same constrained refinement; the decisive test is whether one removes the diagnostic residual family without requiring implausible bonds or profile terms.

The residual reflection sequence now matches the monoclinic Mn₃WO₆ analogue unambiguously: its refined model places strong reflections at 19.91/20.24/20.46, 35.04/35.18, 45.55/46.31, 54.5, 56.51, 59.38, 64.39 and ~70.0°, coincident with the previously unexplained peaks. I’m refining profile and atomic parameters next; the remaining question is quantitative accuracy, especially the Mn₃WO₆ coordinates and fraction derived from an analogue.

The full three-phase refinement is computationally heavier because the Mn₃WO₆ conventional cell contributes hundreds of reflections. It is still running through the bounded size/strain steps; once that stabilizes I’ll refine only symmetry-independent coordinates and verify the saved project by an independent forward replay.

The three-phase assignment is now structurally coherent: MnWO₄, residual triclinic WO₃, and monoclinic Mn₃WO₆. I’m checking the long profile refinement now; after that I’ll refine the atomic models selectively and replay the saved GSAS-II project to ensure every submitted profile and fraction is reproducible.

The profile stage improved the weighted residual from 18.85% to 17.08%, but two instrumental terms ran into bounds, so I’m not accepting that as the final model. I’m separating instrument broadening from phase broadening and using the benchmark’s documented nuisance-model ranges to keep the refinement physically identifiable.

The phase-width refinement is resolving real differences: WO₃ is strongly broadened (about 0.09 μm apparent size), while MnWO₄ is much sharper. The initial common instrumental width was too broad for MnWO₄, which explains the earlier boundary behavior; I’m letting the phase widths settle before releasing the shared instrumental terms.

Selective coordinate refinement is improving all three intensity fits without changing the phase assignment. The conventional phases are now stable; the remaining leverage is in the analogue-derived Mn₃WO₆ coordinates, which I’m refining metals-first so oxygen positions cannot mask an incorrect cation framework.

The unconstrained trial reached a much lower residual, but it also produced a 0.61 Å contact in Mn₃WO₆, so it is diagnostically useful but chemically invalid. I’m discarding that branch and repeating the coordinate refinement from the stable indexed cells with symmetry-aware coordinate limits tied to the documented reference-mismatch scale.

The constrained structural branch passes the contact screen (shortest bonds 1.59–1.84 Å) and already lowers the pre-profile residual from 18.85% to 16.29%. The physical profile refinement is now running with the laboratory’s exact size, strain, instrumental, zero-offset, and specimen-height bounds.

The bounded nuisance fit exposes a likely incompleteness: it can only reproduce several broad residuals by driving the Mn₃WO₆ broadening to limits. Because Mn₃O₄ was the actual precursor and independently improves those regions, I’m testing a four-phase model before finalizing; this is a phase-count check, not an automatic inclusion.

The four-phase replay confirms a real but trace Mn₃O₄ contribution (~0.5 wt%); it lowers the independently recomputed Rwp by 0.33 percentage point and matches its diagnostic 28.9°/36.1° lines. The dominant remaining misfit belongs to the analogue-derived Mn₃WO₆, so I’m widening only that phase’s coordinate adjustment while retaining explicit bond/contact screening.

Allowing a modest analogue-specific adjustment improves the replay to 11.69% while retaining chemically acceptable contacts; the apparent Mn₃O₄ scale then collapses to zero. I’m therefore treating Mn₃O₄ as an unstable trace hypothesis and refining the Mn₃WO₆ metric once more before deciding whether it belongs in the submitted inventory.

The refined three-phase fractions remain close to a 60:20:20 mixture, and phase-common displacement factors improve replay to 10.79%. I’m now checking the finite set of texture axes allowed by the measurement model; each candidate is replayed from the same starting project so only genuine intensity improvement is retained.

The three-phase model remains stable at roughly 58% MnWO₄, 21% WO₃, and 21% Mn₃WO₆. I’m finishing the remaining anisotropic-profile checks, then I’ll freeze and replay the exact GSAS-II project before exporting the deliverables.

The texture scan found no meaningful preferred orientation for MnWO₄, but both minor phases show a measurable [001]-type intensity bias. I’m checking whether those effects remain stable when combined and whether the very broad WO₃ peaks are better described by directional microstrain.

The minor-phase texture terms remain stable when refined together and improve Rwp from 10.79% to about 10.07%. Directional strain and roughness tests either converged back toward isotropic behavior or worsened the fit, so I’m excluding those extra terms. I’m doing one final structural/physical consistency pass on the analogue-derived Mn₃WO₆ model.

The physically regularized Mn₃WO₆ variants are now being compared against the unconstrained intensity optimum. This is the last judgment point: the analogue coordinates should improve the diffraction fit without creating implausibly short bonds; if the gain depends on that distortion, I’ll retain the defensible model and explicitly widen the fraction/structure uncertainty.

The regularized analogue now has chemically sensible separations (minimum Mn–O 2.11 Å, W–O 1.90 Å, O–O 2.19 Å). Its profile penalty is modest; I’m allowing only a final ±0.05 Å oxygen adjustment to see whether the diffraction gain survives without reintroducing short contacts.

The tight oxygen refinement is computationally slower because 27 symmetry-coupled coordinates are being checked against the full profile. The current chemically regularized candidate is already usable at Rwp ≈10.18%; I’ll only accept the tighter refinement if its exported geometry remains clean.

The final physically regularized three-phase model now refines to essentially the mass-balanced 60:20:20 composition. I’m freezing all refinement flags and regenerating the profile from zero cycles; this catches any last discrepancy between a refinement’s cached curve and the submitted forward model.

Completed and validated by a zero-parameter GSAS-II replay.

